{"name":"Melon Orange","hex":"#f08f48","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"melon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Some of the plants of the family Cucurbitaceae grown for food, generally not including the cucumber.\nGenus Cucumis, the true melon (including various cultigens like honeydew and cantaloupes), the horned melon, and others.\nGenus Citrullus, the watermelon and others.\nBenincasa hispida, the winter melon.\nMomordica charantia, the bitter melon.","Genus Cucumis, the true melon (including various cultigens like honeydew and cantaloupes), the horned melon, and others."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light pinkish orange color, like that of melon flesh."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melon"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Melon","blocks":[{"text":"A melon is any of various plants of the family Cucurbitaceae with sweet, edible, and fleshy fruit. It can also specifically refer to Cucumis melo, commonly known as the \"true melon\" or simply \"melon\". The term \"melon\" can apply to both the plant and its fruit. Botanically, a melon is a kind of berry, specifically a \"pepo\". The word melon derives from Latin melopepo, which is the latinization of the Greek μηλοπέπων (mēlopepōn), meaning \"melon\", itself a compound of μῆλον (mēlon), \"apple\", treefruit \" and πέπων (pepōn), amongst others \"a kind of gourd or melon\". Many different cultivars have been produced, particularly of the true melon, such as the cantaloupe and honeydew."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/330px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melon","wikidata":"Q5881191"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange","wikidata":"Q19847194"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/500px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/1280px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/500px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Watermelon and melon in India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWatermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg/500px-Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg/1280px-Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg/500px-Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Horned melon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png/500px-Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png/1280px-Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png/500px-Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Honeydew","